[research] Read Lead-Lag relationship between Bitcoin and Ethereum: Evidence from hourly and daily data (Sifat, Mohamad, Mollah, 2019, Research in International Business and Finance) (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0275531919300522) — Using hourly and daily data, causality between BTC and ETH is largely bi-directional/contemporaneous and intraday traders can barely exploit the hourly price-discovery process to their advantage — evidence AGAINST lag arbitrage, which is why I use BTC only as a regime confirmer, not a lagged predictor.; BTC/ETH Lead-Lag: Resolution-Dependent Direction Reversal on Binance Spot (Alejandro Soto Franco) (https://www.sotofranco.dev/blog/posts/btc-eth-lead-lag) — At ~100ms BTC leads ETH with a statistically significant aggregate bias (z=+5.52), but the per-window signal is noisy and 'not large enough to be reliably exploitable on a per-window basis' — confirming a real but weak lead structure, so I trade the robust beta-amplification rather than the fragile per-window lead.; Price Discovery in Cryptocurrency Markets (arXiv:2506.08718) (https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.08718) — Bitcoin acts as the dominant benchmark for market liquidity and risk appetite and exerts consistent directional influence on large-cap platforms such as Ethereum, showing 5-minute CEX price-discovery leadership — supporting BTC as the market-beta/regime leader that gates the follower trade.
[hypothesis] When the market-beta leader (BTC) prints a volume-confirmed upside breakout scaled by its own recent volatility inside a confirmed uptrend, the higher-beta follower (ETH) extends that move by more than the 0.30% round-trip cost over the next few bars; entering the follower on that leader confirmation and exiting on the leader's short-horizon reversal, a fixed profit/stop, a time stop, or the UTC day boundary beats buy-and-hold and stays inside the 25% drawdown mandate. It is false if follower returns conditional on a confirmed leader breakout do not exceed costs, or if the strategy breaches drawdown.
