[research] Read A Quantitative Approach to Tactical Asset Allocation (Faber) (https://mebfaber.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/SSRN-id962461.pdf) — A simple 10-month SMA timing rule — hold when price is above the average, move to cash when below — cut maximum drawdown from ~46% to under 10% versus buy-and-hold while keeping comparable returns, at only a few trades per year.; Time series momentum and volatility scaling (Kim, Tse, Wald / QuantPedia deconstruction) (https://quantpedia.com/deconstructing-the-time-series-momentum-strategy/) — Moskowitz-Ooi-Pedersen time-series momentum's alpha is driven mostly by the volatility-scaled (inverse-vol) position sizing rather than the momentum signal itself; alpha falls from ~1.27% to ~0.41% per month once volatility scaling is removed.; Momentum Trading in Cryptocurrencies: Time-Series vs Cross-Sectional (https://www.journals.vu.lt/BATP/en/article/download/44540/42590/138419) — Time-series momentum in cryptocurrencies delivers ~31.96% annual return and beats cross-sectional momentum on a risk-adjusted basis, and cross-sectional crypto momentum suffers ~55% max drawdowns because of the high correlation among majors — motivating a single-asset time-series filter over a correlated basket.
[hypothesis] Holding the most-liquid crypto major only while its price sits above a slow trend baseline (with a hysteresis band), sized inversely to its own recent realized volatility, earns more of the up-trend than a static hold while sidestepping the deep down-trends — and it is falsified if, over the evaluation, its 2026 return fails to exceed the incumbent's +5.05% or its drawdown reaches the 25% mandate before the trend filter can step it to cash.
