[research] Read Bitcoin Intraday Time-Series Momentum (Univ. of Reading) (https://centaur.reading.ac.uk/100181/3/21Sep2021Bitcoin%20Intraday%20Time-Series%20Momentum.R2.pdf) — Documents a significant, tradeable intraday time-series momentum effect in Bitcoin — the same short-horizon TSMOM edge the incumbent exploits and that this strategy builds on.; Risks and Returns of Cryptocurrency — Liu & Tsyvinski (NBER w24877) (https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w24877/w24877.pdf) — Current crypto market return predicts future returns up to eight weeks ahead; a one-SD increase in today's Bitcoin return predicts +0.33% next-day return — evidence that time-series momentum persistence is real, motivating the multi-day lookback.; Kaufman's Adaptive Moving Average / Efficiency Ratio — StockCharts ChartSchool (https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-overlays/kaufmans-adaptive-moving-average-kama) — The Efficiency Ratio = price change over a period divided by the summed absolute bar-to-bar movement, ranging 0–1, with higher = more efficient/trending; used to slow down and filter out chop regimes to reduce whipsaw — the basis of my entry gate.; Volatility-Managed Portfolios — Moreira & Muir (2017) (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/jofi.12513) — Scaling exposure inversely to recent realised volatility raises risk-adjusted returns and cuts drawdowns — the rationale for inverse-vol basket weighting and the drawdown-linked de-risk ramp.
[hypothesis] Vol-normalised time-series momentum on liquid crypto majors earns above its 0.30%/round-trip cost ONLY when the trend's path is clean; gating every entry on Kaufman's Efficiency Ratio (displacement/path-length) removes the chop signals that bleed the incumbent, and holding the survivors as a small inverse-vol basket keeps portfolio drawdown under the 25% mandate — it is false if KER-filtered entries do not out-return the same momentum entries taken without the filter, or if the basket still breaches drawdown.
