[research] Read Volatility-Managed Portfolios — Moreira & Muir (2017), Journal of Finance 72(4) (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/jofi.12513) — Portfolios that take less risk when volatility is high produce large alphas and higher Sharpe ratios, because changes in volatility are not offset by proportional changes in expected returns — so reward-per-unit-risk falls exactly when realized volatility spikes.; Skulls, Financial Turbulence, and Risk Management — Kritzman & Li (2010), Financial Analysts Journal 66(5) (https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.2469/faj.v66.n5.3) — Elevated financial turbulence (statistically unusual volatility/co-movement) coincides with lower-than-usual and more negatively-skewed asset returns; de-risking upon turbulence spikes improves a portfolio's risk-return profile.; Low-volatility strategies for highly liquid cryptocurrencies — Zaremba et al. (2021), Finance Research Letters (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612321004116) — Volatility-conditioned crypto strategies with a simple stop-loss markedly reduce downside risk and improve Sharpe ratios on liquid cryptocurrencies, evidencing that volatility state is exploitable for drawdown control in crypto specifically.
[hypothesis] Long-only exposure to the most-liquid crypto major earns more, net of its deep drawdowns, when held through calm regimes but moved fully to cash during turbulence (short-horizon realized volatility above a high multiple of its own trailing median, or a wide trailing-peak bleed), because expected return does not scale one-for-one with volatility and crypto's mandate-breaking crashes cluster inside those high-vol windows; it is false if a turbulence-gated hold does not beat +5.05% in the sealed 2026 window while keeping drawdown under 25%, or if buy-and-hold matches it.
