[research] Read Turn-of-the-month effect in cryptocurrencies (Kayacetin/Gozgor et al., ResearchGate 359327404) (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359327404_Turn-of-the-month_effect_in_cryptocurrencies) — A significant positive turn-of-the-month effect is documented in Bitcoin, Ethereum and Litecoin; the within-the-month (turn) effect is the calendar effect most common across cryptocurrencies.; Equity Returns at the Turn of the Month (Xu & McConnell, Purdue) (https://business.purdue.edu/faculty/mcconnell/publications/Equity-Returns-at-the-Turn-of-the-Month.pdf) — Over 1926-2005 all of the excess market return occurs during the four-day turn-of-the-month interval (last trading day through the first three of the next month); returns outside it are indistinguishable from zero.; Revisiting seasonality in cryptocurrencies (Finance Research Letters, S1544612324004598) (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612324004598) — Cryptocurrency calendar anomalies are asset-specific and mostly weak, but the within-/turn-of-the-month effect is the one pattern that appears common across the major coins.; Seasonal and Calendar Effects and the Price Efficiency of Cryptocurrencies (Finance Research Letters, S1544612321003597) (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1544612321003597) — Anomalies found in Bitcoin do not generally hold for other cryptos and vice versa, with the within-the-month effect being the only effect common to all cryptocurrencies examined.
[hypothesis] Bitcoin earns a disproportionate share of its return in the turn-of-the-month window (last ~2 calendar days of a month through the first ~3 of the next), driven by recurring month-boundary inflows — salary/DCA buying, fund rebalancing, and monthly futures/options settlement; holding BTC only inside that window and sitting in cash otherwise beats a buy-and-hold-a-trend rule net of costs. It is false if 2026 window returns do not exceed the ~0.30% round-trip toll times the ~2 trades/month it incurs.
